  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Empowering CBO&apos;s to develop sustainable community-based solutions to poverty</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over eight million people live a precarious existence in the overcrowded slums of Ethiopia&apos;s largest cities. As many as one in three have no access to piped water and decent toilets. Many live in substandard accommodation, with limited opportunities to make a living.  Too many children &#x2013; especially girls and orphans &#x2013; can&apos;t afford to go to school. The project will work in the slums of five Ethiopian cities.  They will invest in local community organisations that are based in the slums.  These groups will help to improve living conditions in the slums by upgrading housing, installing pit latrines and water kiosks.  They will run community schools for disadvantaged children.  They will promote urban agriculture so families can sell the produce in local markets.  They will invest in vocational and business training for local groups of women and young people who want to set up their own businesses.   The groups will also engage with local government to ensure that slum upgrading programmes are responsive to local people&apos;s needs and aspirations. As a result of this work, tens of thousands of slum dwellers will have better living conditions, which will reduce the incidence of preventable diseases. Children will receive a decent basic education and families will have a more secure way of earning a living.  Local governments will be more accountable to local people, and systems will be in place to ensure local people are consulted in slum development programmes.</narrative>
  </description>
